    correctAnswer: 1,
    explanation: 'A cost-aware scoring plugin caches instance pricing, factors it into node scores, and balances cost against other scheduling objectives like resource fit and topology.',
    codeSnippet: '// Cost-aware scheduling plugin:\ntype CostAwareScorer struct {\n    // Cached pricing data (updated every 5 minutes)\n    priceCache sync.Map // nodeType -> pricePerHour\n}\n\nfunc (c *CostAwareScorer) Score(ctx context.Context, state *framework.CycleState,\n    pod *v1.Pod, nodeName string) (int64, *framework.Status) {\n\n    node := getNode(nodeName)\n    instanceType := node.Labels["node.kubernetes.io/instance-type"]\n\n    price, _ := c.priceCache.Load(instanceType)\n    pricePerHour := price.(float64)\n\n    // Normalize to 0-100 score (lower cost = higher score)\n    maxPrice := c.getMaxPrice() // Most expensive instance type\n    minPrice := c.getMinPrice() // Cheapest instance type\n\n    // Linear scoring: cheapest gets 100, most expensive gets 0\n    score := int64((maxPrice - pricePerHour) / (maxPrice - minPrice) * 100)\n\n    return score, framework.NewStatus(framework.Success)\n}\n\n// Plugin weight in scheduler config:\n// profiles:\n//   - plugins:\n//       score:\n//         enabled:\n//           - name: CostAwareScorer\n//             weight: 30  # 30% influence on final score\n//           - name: NodeResourcesFit\n//             weight: 50  # 50% influence (most important)\n//           - name: NodeAffinity\n//             weight: 20  # 20% influence',
